WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.260
Hello!

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:06.390
I am David Kaplan and I am not an expert,
but I hope in this series to learn as much

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:14.140
as possible and explain it to you the best
way I can.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:18.071
Imagine being in the desert, a desert which
is very uniform.

00:00:18.071 --> 00:00:21.980
You look in any direction, you see the same
thing.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:26.340
That, seeing the same thing, means there’s
a symmetry in that state.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.010
It’s a rotational symmetry.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:34.190
Now take the desert and add roads and casinos,
lots of stuff.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:39.559
Now every direction you look it looks completely
different, so that is a situation where an

00:00:39.559 --> 00:00:45.299
underlying symmetry exists but the state of
the system breaks it.

00:00:45.299 --> 00:00:48.239
This is something that occurs with the laws
of nature.

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:55.280
It is these broken symmetries — the underlying,
hidden symmetries in the laws — that allow

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.760
us to discover deeper and deeper aspects of
forces and particles.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.320
The Higgs boson is a great example of that.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:11.580
The Higgs boson was predicted as something
that breaks a symmetry of the theory of fundamental

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.630
particles, called the standard model.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:20.480
The symmetry that was broken has effects like
making the electron and the neutrino completely

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.510
different.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.090
The electron is a particle that lives in the
atom.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.450
It has electric charge.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:28.300
It’s very important for all of matter.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:33.410
And the neutrino moves through space near
the speed of light, fills the universe but

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:35.980
otherwise doesn’t seem very important.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:41.180
How could these two particles have been roughly
the same particle in the early universe?

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.300
That’s because, we’ve discovered, there’s
a symmetry that connects them.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:50.950
And at some point, as the temperature of the
universe cooled, that symmetry broke and the

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:54.030
breaking was due to the Higgs mechanism.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:59.890
And the theory that predicted that breaking
also predicted the particle that was just

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.330
discovered a few years ago.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:07.690
So the insights — which are “What are
the symmetries that guide and structure the

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:09.209
fundamental laws?”

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:14.989
— lead to discoveries like the Higgs and
a deeper understanding about how all particles

00:02:14.989 --> 00:02:15.920
and forces work.

